      <h2 id="quick-answer" class="text-lg font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-3">Quick answer</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2.5 text-[0.98rem] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">
        <li><strong>Does Fitbit track stress?</strong> Yes, indirectly — from heart rate, HRV, skin sweat (EDA) and sleep. It does not measure cortisol.</li>
        <li><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Google Health app (formerly the Fitbit app) → <strong>Health</strong> tab → <strong>Mental wellbeing</strong> → <strong>Resilience</strong> or <strong>Body responses</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Body Responses</strong> flag a sudden rise in fight-or-flight activity on Sense devices and Pixel Watch 2 and later.</li>
        <li><strong>Good EDA score?</strong> Fitbit doesn't define one. Fewer EDA responses typically means calmer.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- On this page -->
    <nav class="app-card overflow-hidden mb-6" aria-label="On this page">
      <div class="px-5 pt-4 pb-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-widest font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]">On this page</div>
      <ol class="app-list">
        {toc.map((item) => (
          <li><a href={`#${item.id}`} class="app-row text-[0.98rem] font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><span>{item.label}</span><span class="app-chevron" aria-hidden="true">›</span></a></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class={proseCard}>
      <p class="!text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] text-sm sm:text-base"><strong>2026 name changes:</strong> on May 19, 2026 the Fitbit app became the <strong>Google Health app</strong>, and the Stress Management Score was renamed <strong>Resilience</strong>, now shown as Optimal, Balanced or Low instead of a number (<a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/17068213?hl=en">Google Health Help — what's new</a>). Your Fitbit device works the same way; only the app labels moved.</p>

      <h2 id="body-responses">What are Body Responses on Fitbit?</h2>
      <p><strong>A Body Response is Fitbit's notice that your body is showing signs that could be stress.</strong> On supported devices such as Fitbit Sense 2, a continuous electrodermal activity (cEDA) sensor watches for tiny changes in skin sweat, and Fitbit combines them with heart rate, heart-rate variability and skin temperature. When those signals shift together, the app can notify you and ask you to log how you feel (<a href="https://blog.google/products/fitbit/how-we-trained-fitbits-body-response-feature-to-detect-stress/">Google's explanation of the feature</a>).</p>
      <p>Google's help page defines it as a "sudden and rapid increase in autonomic nervous system activity" and notes that Body Responses don't track your overall perceived stress — only those sudden spikes. They're available on the Fitbit Sense series and Google Pixel Watch 2 and later; if you've turned on notifications, the alert arrives roughly 10 minutes after the response (<a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en">Google Health Help — Manage stress &amp; mindfulness</a>).</p>
      <h3>What does "Body Responses" mean on Fitbit?</h3>
      <p>A Body Response means your nervous system reacted to something. That can be stress, but also exercise, excitement, heat or caffeine, which is why Fitbit asks you to label it. It is not a cortisol reading. The notifications are optional: in the Google Health app, tap Connections, then your device, then Notifications. To review past responses, go to Health → Mental wellbeing → Body responses and switch between the day and week views.</p>

      <h2 id="how-to-track-stress">How to track stress levels on Fitbit</h2>
      <p>Fitbit's stress features live in the Google Health app (the renamed Fitbit app). Step by step:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Wear a compatible device overnight and let it sync.</strong> Resilience works on Charge 4, 5 and 6, Inspire 2 and 3, Luxe, the Sense series, Versa 2, 3 and 4, Pixel Watch 2 and later, and Google Fitbit Air.</li>
        <li><strong>Open your daily Resilience rating:</strong> tap the <strong>Health</strong> tab, then under <em>Focus areas</em> tap <strong>Mental wellbeing</strong> → <strong>Resilience</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Check Body Responses</strong> (Sense series, Pixel Watch 2 and later): from <strong>Mental wellbeing</strong>, tap <strong>Body responses</strong> for the day and week views. On the device, you can log your mood when an alert arrives.</li>
        <li><strong>Run an EDA scan</strong> (Charge 5, Charge 6, Sense): open the <strong>EDA Scan</strong> app on the device and stay still while you hold it. On Charge 5 you hold the sides of the tracker with your thumb and index finger; the summary shows your number of EDA responses and your heart rate at the start and end (<a href="https://www.fitbit.com/content/assets/help/manuals/manual_charge_5_en_US.pdf">Fitbit Charge 5 user manual</a>). These results now stay on the device.</li>
        <li><strong>Read the trend, not the day.</strong> Look at a week or two of ratings alongside your <a href="/fitbit/hrv/">Fitbit HRV</a> and sleep before drawing conclusions.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Navigation and device lists are from <a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en">Google Health Help</a>; menus can differ by app version and region.</p>

      <h2 id="does-fitbit-track-stress">Does Fitbit track stress?</h2>
      <p><strong>Yes, but indirectly.</strong> Fitbit tracks the physical signs of stress — not stress itself, and not any stress hormone. Every compatible device contributes heart rate, HRV, exertion and sleep to the daily Resilience rating; devices with an EDA sensor add skin-sweat data, and Sense devices and Pixel Watch 2 and later also flag Body Responses in real time.</p>

      <h2 id="how-fitbit-measures-stress">How does Fitbit measure stress?</h2>
      <p>Stress is a whole-body response coordinated by your autonomic nervous system. When you're under load — physical or mental — the sympathetic ("fight or flight") branch speeds the heart, tightens the beat-to-beat rhythm, and increases sweat gland activity. When you recover, the parasympathetic ("rest and digest") branch takes over and reverses those changes. Fitbit can't see the hormones behind this, but it can see the downstream physiological signals, and that's what its stress features are built on.</p>
      <h3>The four signals Fitbit reads</h3>
      <p><strong>1. Heart-rate variability (HRV).</strong> HRV is the variation in time between consecutive heartbeats. Counterintuitively, more variability is usually healthier — it reflects strong parasympathetic (vagal) tone. Falling HRV is one of the most consistent physiological markers of stress load in the research literature. Fitbit measures HRV overnight, when outside factors like caffeine and movement are minimized. See <a href="/fitbit/hrv/">our full guide to Fitbit HRV</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>2. Resting and elevated heart rate.</strong> A resting heart rate that runs higher than your baseline, or that stays elevated when you'd expect it to settle, is a classic sign the sympathetic system is dominant. Fitbit tracks this continuously via the optical (PPG) sensor on the wrist.</p>
      <p><strong>3. Electrodermal activity (EDA).</strong> Skin sweat changes driven by the sympathetic nervous system — covered in detail below.</p>
      <p><strong>4. Sleep.</strong> Sleep both reflects and regulates stress. Short or fragmented sleep raises next-day physiological arousal, and Fitbit folds sleep quality and quantity directly into its stress and readiness scoring.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Mid-article CTA -->
    <aside class="my-6 app-sky rounded-3xl overflow-hidden px-5 pt-6 pb-6 text-center" aria-labelledby="fitbit-cta">
