      <p class="mt-2 mb-5 text-[color:var(--color-ink)]/75 leading-relaxed text-pretty">
        Cortisol+ reads these signals from Apple Health on iPhone and Apple Watch, or from Health Connect on Android. Fitbit support via Health Connect is on the way — it isn't a shipped feature yet. Its score is a wellness estimate, not a cortisol test; <a href="/how-it-works/" class="underline font-semibold">see the methodology</a>.
      </p>
      <GetAppButton />
    </aside>

    <div class={proseCard}>
      <h2 id="eda-sensor">What is the EDA sensor on Fitbit?</h2>
      <p>EDA stands for <strong>electrodermal activity</strong>: "tiny changes in the sweat level of your skin," in Google's words. Sweat gland activity is controlled by the sympathetic nervous system, so these changes can show trends in how your body responds to stress (<a href="https://www.fitbit.com/content/assets/help/manuals/manual_charge_5_en_US.pdf">Fitbit Charge 5 manual</a>). On devices with an EDA sensor — such as Fitbit Sense and Charge 5 and later — you can run an EDA Scan by holding the device still with your hand or fingers. More responses during a scan can indicate a more reactive stress state.</p>
      <p>Fitbit uses EDA in two ways. <strong>Spot-check EDA scans</strong> run on demand on Charge 5, Charge 6 and Sense; Google notes that these results now stay on the device and no longer sync to the phone app. <strong>Continuous EDA (cEDA)</strong> on the Sense series and Pixel Watch 2 and later runs in the background and powers Body Responses (<a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en">Google Health Help</a>).</p>

      <h2 id="good-eda-score">What is a good EDA score on Fitbit?</h2>
      <p><strong>Fitbit doesn't define a "good" EDA score or a target number of responses.</strong> Its only guidance is that "while your EDA response may vary, you should typically expect fewer EDA responses the calmer you are" (<a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en">Google Health Help</a>). Movement, noise and temperature changes can also trigger responses.</p>
      <p>The practical way to use the number: scan at a similar time, in a similar setting, sitting still, and compare against your own previous scans. A lower count than usual suggests a calmer state; one high scan after coffee or a walk tells you little.</p>

      <h2 id="resilience">Resilience: the old Stress Management Score</h2>
      <p>Each morning, compatible devices produce a daily rating of your capacity to handle strain. Until May 2026 this was the <a href="/fitbit/stress-management-score/">Stress Management Score from 1 to 100</a>; it is now called <strong>Resilience</strong> and shown as Optimal, Balanced or Low. Per Google, it's built from more than ten factors grouped into three categories: <strong>responsiveness</strong> (heart rate, HRV, and EDA data), <strong>exertion balance</strong> (recent exercise versus your longer-term activity), and <strong>sleep patterns</strong> (quality and quantity of your sleep). Resilience is not a Premium-only feature.</p>

      <h3>The Readiness Score</h3>
      <p>Fitbit's Readiness Score answers a different question: how prepared is your body for exertion today? It combines your <strong>HRV</strong>, <strong>resting heart rate</strong>, and recent <strong>sleep</strong> into a 1–100 value, banded Low (29 or below), Moderate (30–64), and High (65 or above). A low readiness score after a stretch of poor sleep or high stress is a nudge to prioritize recovery rather than a hard workout.</p>

      <h2 id="limits">What Fitbit stress tracking does — and doesn't — tell you</h2>
      <p>What it does well: gives you an objective, trend-able signal of autonomic arousal that you can watch over weeks. Falling HRV, a creeping resting heart rate, and reactive EDA scans together paint a real picture of accumulating strain.</p>
      <p>What it can't do: measure cortisol or any hormone, tell "good" stress from "bad" (a workout and an argument can look identical to the sensors), or explain the cause of a change. It's a thermometer, not a diagnosis. Treat a single low rating as noise and a two-week trend as signal.</p>

      <h2>Where cortisol comes in</h2>
      <p>The hormone most people mean when they say "stress" is cortisol, and no consumer wearable — Fitbit included — measures it directly. What wearables track are the <em>proxies</em> for cortisol's downstream effects, chiefly HRV. We cover the honest version of this in <a href="/fitbit/cortisol/">Can Fitbit track cortisol?</a> Cortisol+ is bringing its cortisol-pattern estimation to Fitbit data via Google Health Connect; today the app works with Apple Watch through Apple Health and with Android through Health Connect.</p>

